1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,020
 So far, our queries have been done in a single forward pass. Given the query, call the right tool with the right parameters and get back the response.

2
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:18,680
 But this is still quite limiting. What if the user asks a complex question consisting of multiple steps or a vague question that needs clarification?

3
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:29,520
 In this lesson, you will learn how to define a complete agent reasoning loop. Instead of tool calling in a single shot setting, an agent is able to reason over tools in multiple steps.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,560
 You will use the function calling agent implementation, which is an agent that natively integrates with the function calling capabilities of LLMs.

5
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:39,600
 All right, let's have some fun.

6
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:43,760
 To get started, we'll set up our opening IKey.

7
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:48,700
 And then we'll set up an import Nest AsyncIO.

8
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:54,200
 For now, we use the same meta-GPT paper as the previous two lessons.

9
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:57,260
 In the next lesson, we'll expand to the multi-doc setting.

10
00:00:57,260 --> 00:01:02,660
 And we will also set up the auto-retrieval vector search tool and the summarization tool from the last lesson.

11
00:01:02,660 --> 00:01:08,860
 To make this more concise, we've packaged this into a single line of code that you can just import from the utils module.

12
00:01:08,860 --> 00:01:11,800
 We now set up our function calling agent.

13
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:17,260
 As with the previous notebooks, we use GPT 3.5 Turbo as our LLM.

14
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:19,740
 We will then define our agent.

15
00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:27,560
 In LLM index, an agent consists of two main components, an agent worker as well as an agent runner.

16
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:33,640
 Think of an agent worker that's responsible for executing the next step of a given agent.

17
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:46,520
 And an agent runner is the overall task dispatcher, which is responsible for creating a task, orchestrating runs of agent workers on top of a given task, and being able to return back the final response to the user.

18
00:01:48,860 --> 00:01:54,420
 So we import both function calling agent worker from LLM index and also the agent runner.

19
00:01:54,420 --> 00:02:01,940
 And for the function calling agent worker, we pass in the two set of tools, the vector tool as well as the summary tool.

20
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:08,840
 We also pass in the LLM and set verbose equal to true to look at the intermediate outputs.

21
00:02:08,840 --> 00:02:21,320
 Think about the function calling agent worker's primary responsibility as given the existing conversation history, memory, and any past state, along with the current user input.

22
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:28,880
 Use function calling to decide the next tool to call call, call that tool, and decide whether or not to return a final response.

23
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:34,740
 The overall agent interface is behind the agent runner, and that's what we're going to use to query the agent.

24
00:02:34,740 --> 00:02:38,580
 We'll first ask this question.

25
00:02:38,580 --> 00:02:44,100
 Tell me about the agent roles in MetaGPT, and then how they communicate with each other.

26
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:55,260
 So let's trace through the outputs of this agent.

27
00:02:55,260 --> 00:03:01,340
 We see that the agent is able to break down this overall question into steps.

28
00:03:01,340 --> 00:03:08,240
 So the first part is asking about agent roles in MetaGPT, and it calls the summary tool to answer this question.

29
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:12,160
 Now, a quick note is that the summary tool isn't necessarily the most precise.

30
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:20,800
 You could argue that the vector tool will actually give you back a more concise set of context that better represents this relevant pieces of text that you're looking for.

31
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:23,700
 However, a summary tool is still a reasonable tool for the job.

32
00:03:24,040 --> 00:03:33,100
 And, of course, more powerful models like 4Turbo or Claude3, Sonnet, or Opus might be able to pick the more precise vector tool to help answer this question.

33
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:36,240
 In any case, we see that we're able to get back the output.

34
00:03:36,240 --> 00:03:42,720
 The agent roles in MetaGPT include product manager, architect, project manager, QA engineer, etc.

35
00:03:42,720 --> 00:03:52,300
 And then it uses this to perform chain of thought to then trigger the next question, which is communication between agent roles in MetaGPT.

36
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:56,440
 We see we're able to get back an answer about that, too.

37
00:03:56,440 --> 00:04:00,160
 You know, communication between agent roles in MetaGPT is structured and efficient.

38
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:04,820
 And we're able to combine this entire conversation history to generate a final response.

39
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:10,140
 Communication in MetaGPT, the agent roles include these.

40
00:04:10,140 --> 00:04:14,040
 They collaborate in a sequential manner to follow standard operating procedures.

41
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:18,540
 And they use a shared message role and subscription mechanism.

42
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:25,360
 When you run a multi-step query like this, you want to make sure that you're actually able to trace the sources.

43
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:33,080
 And so, luckily, similar to the previous lessons, you're able to look at response.source nodes to take a look at the content of these nodes.

44
00:04:33,080 --> 00:04:41,020
 So, this allows you to, for instance, inspect the content of the first source node that's retrieved, which is just the first page of the paper.

45
00:04:41,700 --> 00:04:47,080
 Calling agent.query allows you to query the agent in a one-off manner, but does not preserve state.

46
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:50,200
 So, now let's try maintaining conversation history over time.

47
00:04:51,300 --> 00:04:55,160
 The agent is able to maintain chats in a conversational memory buffer.

48
00:04:55,160 --> 00:05:03,500
 The memory module can be customized, but by default, it's a flat list of items that's a rolling buffer, depending on the size of the context window of the LLN.

49
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:13,980
 Therefore, when the agent decides to use a tool, it not only uses the current chat, but also the previous conversation history to take the next step or perform the next action.

50
00:05:15,980 --> 00:05:18,340
 So, instead of query, we'll do agent.chat.

51
00:05:18,340 --> 00:05:22,600
 The first thing we'll ask is, tell me about the evaluation data sets used.

52
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:33,200
 Here, we see that it uses a summary tool to ask, what are the evaluation data sets used in MetaGPT?

53
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:39,520
 And we see the eval data sets used include human eval, mvpp, and software dev.

54
00:05:43,800 --> 00:05:49,120
 You'll see an example of this ability to maintain conversation history, because we'll ask a follow-up question.

55
00:05:49,120 --> 00:05:52,240
 Tell me the results over one of the above data sets.

56
00:05:52,240 --> 00:05:57,400
 Obviously, to know what the above data sets are, you have to have that stored in the conversation history somewhere.

57
00:05:57,400 --> 00:06:01,720
 So, let's run this.

58
00:06:01,720 --> 00:06:08,520
 And it's able to translate this query, plus conversation history, into a query on the vector tool, actually.

59
00:06:08,900 --> 00:06:13,920
 And it asks, results over the human eval data set, which is one of the eval data sets that are used.

60
00:06:13,920 --> 00:06:16,860
 And it's able to give you back a final answer.

61
00:06:16,860 --> 00:06:21,320
 So, we just provided a nice high-level interface for interacting with an agent.

62
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:28,280
 In this next section, we'll show you capabilities that let you step through and control the agent in a much more granular fashion.

63
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:34,440
 This allows you to not only create a higher-level research assistant over your RAG pipelines, but also debug and control it.

64
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:42,440
 Some of the benefits here include greater debuggability into the execution of each step, as well as steerability by allowing you to inject user feedback.

65
00:06:42,440 --> 00:06:46,880
 Having this low-level agent interface is powerful for two main reasons.

66
00:06:47,280 --> 00:06:49,100
 The first is debuggability.

67
00:06:49,100 --> 00:06:56,600
 If you're a developer building an agent, you might want to have greater transparency and visibility into what's actually going on under the hood,

68
00:06:56,600 --> 00:07:00,020
 especially, say, if your agent isn't working the first time around.

69
00:07:00,020 --> 00:07:05,560
 Then you can actually go in, trace through the execution of the agent, see where it's failing,

70
00:07:05,560 --> 00:07:11,720
 and actually try out different inputs to see if that actually modifies the agent execution into a correct response.

71
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:16,520
 Another reason why this could be useful is to actually enable richer UXs,

72
00:07:16,520 --> 00:07:20,000
 where you're building a product experience around this core agent capability.

73
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:26,060
 For instance, let's say you want to listen to human feedback in the middle of agent execution,

74
00:07:26,060 --> 00:07:29,980
 as opposed to only after the agent execution is complete for a given task.

75
00:07:30,460 --> 00:07:36,640
 Then you can imagine creating some sort of async queue where you're able to listen to input from humans

76
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:39,000
 throughout the middle of agent execution.

77
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:45,420
 And if human input actually comes in, you can actually interrupt and modify the execution of an agent

78
00:07:45,420 --> 00:07:51,140
 as it's going on through a larger task, as opposed to having to wait until the agent task is complete.

79
00:07:51,140 --> 00:07:56,000
 So we'll start by defining our agent again through the function calling agent worker,

80
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:57,440
 as well as the agent runner setup.

81
00:07:57,440 --> 00:07:59,860
 And then we start using the low-level API.

82
00:08:00,460 --> 00:08:03,100
 We'll first create a task object from the user query.

83
00:08:03,100 --> 00:08:07,060
 And then we'll start running through steps for even interjecting our own.

84
00:08:07,060 --> 00:08:10,460
 Now let's try executing a single step of this task.

85
00:08:10,460 --> 00:08:12,560
 So let's create a task for this agent,

86
00:08:12,560 --> 00:08:15,800
 and we'll use the same question we used in the very first part of this lesson.

87
00:08:15,800 --> 00:08:18,300
 Tell me about the agent roles in MetaGPT,

88
00:08:18,300 --> 00:08:20,000
 and then how they communicate with each other.

89
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:23,340
 This will return a task object, which contains the input,

90
00:08:23,340 --> 00:08:25,980
 as well as additional state in the task object.

91
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:29,920
 And now let's try executing a single step of this task.

92
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:34,280
 We'll call agent.run step task.taskid,

93
00:08:34,280 --> 00:08:39,240
 and the agent will execute a step of that task through the task ID

94
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:40,600
 and give you back a step output.

95
00:08:46,120 --> 00:08:50,840
 We'll see that it calls the summary tool with the input agent roles in MetaGPT,

96
00:08:50,840 --> 00:08:52,720
 which is the very first part of this question,

97
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:54,440
 and then it stops there.

98
00:08:54,440 --> 00:09:01,580
 When we inspect the logs and the output of the agent,

99
00:09:01,580 --> 00:09:04,060
 we see that the first part was actually executed.

100
00:09:04,760 --> 00:09:08,520
 So we call agent.getCompleted steps on the task ID,

101
00:09:08,520 --> 00:09:11,740
 and we're able to look at numCompleted for the task.

102
00:09:11,740 --> 00:09:17,080
 We see that one step has been completed,

103
00:09:17,080 --> 00:09:18,880
 and this is the current output so far.

104
00:09:18,880 --> 00:09:24,340
 We can also take a look at any upcoming steps for the agent,

105
00:09:24,340 --> 00:09:27,500
 and we can do this through agent.getUpcomingSteps.

106
00:09:29,920 --> 00:09:33,100
 Again, we pass the task.taskID into the agent,

107
00:09:33,100 --> 00:09:36,680
 and we're able to print out the number of upcoming steps for the task.

108
00:09:36,680 --> 00:09:39,000
 We see that it's also one,

109
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,300
 and we're able to look at a task step object

110
00:09:41,300 --> 00:09:44,280
 with a task ID as well as an existing input.

111
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:46,200
 This input is currently none

112
00:09:46,200 --> 00:09:48,100
 because the way the agent works

113
00:09:48,100 --> 00:09:50,900
 is it actually just auto-generates action

114
00:09:50,900 --> 00:09:52,340
 from the conversation history

115
00:09:52,340 --> 00:09:55,100
 and doesn't need to generate an additional external input.

116
00:09:55,100 --> 00:09:57,240
 The nice thing about this debugging interface

117
00:09:57,240 --> 00:10:00,160
 is that if you wanted to pause execution now, you can.

118
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:02,280
 You can take the intermediate results

119
00:10:02,280 --> 00:10:03,780
 without completing the agent flow.

120
00:10:03,780 --> 00:10:05,660
 But let's keep going,

121
00:10:05,660 --> 00:10:07,920
 and let's run the next two steps

122
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:10,060
 and actually try injecting user input.

123
00:10:10,060 --> 00:10:12,940
 So let's actually ask,

124
00:10:12,940 --> 00:10:17,120
 what about how agents share information as user input?

125
00:10:17,120 --> 00:10:19,980
 This was not part of the original task query,

126
00:10:19,980 --> 00:10:21,740
 but by injecting this,

127
00:10:21,740 --> 00:10:23,800
 this can actually modify agent execution

128
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:25,480
 to give you back the result that you want.

129
00:10:25,920 --> 00:10:30,820
 We'll see that we added the user message to memory

130
00:10:30,820 --> 00:10:32,880
 and that the next call here

131
00:10:32,880 --> 00:10:35,840
 is how agents actually share information in meta-GPT.

132
00:10:35,840 --> 00:10:41,400
 And we see here that it's able to give back the response.

133
00:10:41,400 --> 00:10:45,240
 The overall task is roughly complete,

134
00:10:45,240 --> 00:10:47,020
 and we just need to run one final step

135
00:10:47,020 --> 00:10:48,000
 to synthesize the answer.

136
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,860
 And to double-check that this output is the last step,

137
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,140
 we just need to do step output dot is last.

138
00:10:53,140 --> 00:10:59,080
 So we see we're able to get back the answer

139
00:10:59,080 --> 00:11:01,620
 about how agents in meta-GPT share information,

140
00:11:01,620 --> 00:11:04,020
 and this is indeed the last step.

141
00:11:04,020 --> 00:11:07,920
 To translate this into an agent response,

142
00:11:07,920 --> 00:11:09,300
 similar to what you've seen

143
00:11:09,300 --> 00:11:11,320
 in some of the previous notebook cells,

144
00:11:11,320 --> 00:11:12,980
 then all you have to do

145
00:11:12,980 --> 00:11:15,980
 is call response equals agent dot finalized response.

146
00:11:15,980 --> 00:11:20,220
 And we're able to get back the final answer.

147
00:11:20,220 --> 00:11:26,960
 So that's it for lesson three.

148
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:28,940
 You've learned both about the high-level interface

149
00:11:28,940 --> 00:11:29,440
 for an agent,

150
00:11:29,440 --> 00:11:31,520
 as well as a low-level debugging interface.

151
00:11:31,520 --> 00:11:33,280
 And in the next lesson,

152
00:11:33,280 --> 00:11:34,780
 we'll show you how to build an agent

153
00:11:34,780 --> 00:11:35,860
 over multiple documents.

154
